Subject: [PATCH 21/21] libc/vsprintf: %s precision is a MAXIMUM, not a minimum
Arch-tested: x86 amd64 ppc

libc/vsprintf.c's %s case treated `precision` as a floor:

    #if 0
        len = strnlen(s, precision);
    #else
        len = mstrlen(s);
        if( precision > len )
            len = precision;
    #endif

C99 7.19.6.1 says the opposite -- precision on %s is the MAXIMUM number of
bytes to write, and the argument need not be NUL-terminated within them. So

    %.3s   on "abcdef"   printed all six characters
    %.10s  on "abc"      produced TEN bytes, seven of them past the terminator
    %.0s   on "abc"      printed "abc"
    %8.3s  on "abcdef"   confused precision with field width

The correct call was sitting one line above under an `#if 0`, and
libc/string.c:214's strnlen() is and always was correct. This is a live line
that somebody disabled, not a missing implementation. The `-1` sentinel for
"no precision given" keeps the untouched path untouched.

FOUND BY THE PREVIOUS PATCH. Patch 20 added a diagnostic to end a silence and
used `%.96s` to clip a Forth fragment; it printed a mangled half-line. A
message written to end a silence, silenced by the printf it was written with.

IN-TREE VICTIMS: fs/hfsplus/hfsp_record.c prints a FourCC that is NOT
NUL-terminated with `%4.4s`, twice. Measured with the defect in place, a 4-byte
field followed by "XYZ!" printed ABCDXYZ! -- eight characters where four were
asked for, from a debug path that walks off the end of a struct field.

THE FIXTURES RUN IN THE FIRMWARE, not on the host, and that is deliberate:
kernel/bootstrap.c #defines printk to the HOST printf under BOOTSTRAP, so a
host harness would exercise glibc and report on OpenBIOS. `test-printf-report`'s
neighbour `test-printf-precision` is bound in libopenbios/init.c -- one
definition for all three arches -- and prints one line per case plus a ratio:

    printf-precision: clip      want[abc](3)       got[abc](3)       OK
    printf-precision: no-extend want[abc](3)       got[abc](3)       OK
    printf-precision: fourcc    want[ABCD](4)      got[ABCD](4)      OK
    printf-precision: bare      want[abc](3)       got[abc](3)       OK
    printf-precision: zero      want[](0)          got[](0)          OK
    printf-precision: left      want[abc     |](9) got[abc     |](9) OK
    printf-precision: right     want[     abc|](9) got[     abc|](9) OK
    printf-precision: 7/7 ok

Six of the seven were wrong before this patch. The seventh -- bare `%s`, no
precision -- is the must-NOT-break control: a "fix" that pushed the
no-precision path through strnlen(s, -1) would still pass the other six.

NEGATIVE CONTROL, run not reasoned: the old two lines put back, amd64 rebuilt,
and the fixture reported 1/7 with `bare` the only survivor.

AND THE CONTROL FOUND A HOLE IN THE FIXTURE, which is the part worth keeping.
The first version compared with strcmp() alone, and strcmp STOPS AT THE FIRST
NUL -- so `%.10s` on "abc" wrote "abc\0" plus six bytes of whatever followed,
and strcmp(got, "abc") said EQUAL. The case that most directly demonstrates an
over-read was the one case the fixture could not see; it reported 2/7 and
called `no-extend` a pass. vsnprintf() returns str-buf, what it actually
produced, so the length is now checked beside the bytes:

    printf-precision: no-extend want[abc](3) got[abc](10) BAD

and the control reports 1/7. An assertion that can only see up to a NUL is not
an assertion about a function whose bug is writing past one.

NOT COVERED: precision on conversions other than %s (%.*d and friends go
through number(), a different code path), and vsnprintf's truncation behaviour
at the buffer edge. Named rather than counted as tested.

diff --git a/libc/vsprintf.c b/libc/vsprintf.c
--- a/libc/vsprintf.c
+++ b/libc/vsprintf.c
@@ -268,13 +268,41 @@ int vsnprintf(char *buf, size_t size, const char *fmt, va_list args)
 				if ((unsigned long)s < PAGE_SIZE)
 					s = "<NULL>";
 
-#if 0
-                                len = strnlen(s, precision);
-#else
-                                len = mstrlen(s);
-                                if( precision > len )
-                                    len = precision;
-#endif
+				/* PRECISION ON %s IS A MAXIMUM (C99 7.19.6.1):
+				 * print AT MOST `precision` bytes, and the
+				 * argument need not be NUL-terminated within
+				 * them. It is not a minimum, and it is not a
+				 * field width -- the padding below is what
+				 * handles width.
+				 *
+				 * What was here did the opposite:
+				 *
+				 *     len = mstrlen(s);
+				 *     if( precision > len ) len = precision;
+				 *
+				 * so `%.96s` on a 13-byte string printed 96
+				 * bytes, 83 of them PAST THE TERMINATOR, and
+				 * `%.3s` on "abcdef" printed all six. The
+				 * correct call was sitting directly above it
+				 * under an `#if 0`, and libc/string.c:214's
+				 * strnlen() is and always was correct -- so
+				 * this is a live line that was disabled, not
+				 * a missing implementation.
+				 *
+				 * Found 2026-08-26 when a NEW diagnostic in
+				 * libopenbios/bindings.c used `%.96s` to clip
+				 * a Forth fragment and printed a mangled
+				 * half-line: a message added to end a silence,
+				 * silenced by the printf it was written with.
+				 *
+				 * fs/hfsplus/hfsp_record.c's two `%4.4s` over
+				 * a non-terminated FourCC are the only other
+				 * callers in the tree, and both could print
+				 * more than four characters. -1 means "no
+				 * precision given" (set at the `.` parse
+				 * above), and that path is unchanged. */
+				len = (precision < 0) ? mstrlen(s)
+						      : (int)strnlen(s, (size_t)precision);
 				if (!(flags & LEFT)) {
 					while (len < field_width--) {
 						if (str <= end)
--- a/libopenbios/init.c
+++ b/libopenbios/init.c
@@ -16,6 +16,8 @@
 
 #include "config.h"
 #include "libc/byteorder.h"
+#include "libc/string.h"
+#include "libc/vsprintf.h"
 #include "libopenbios/openbios.h"
 #include "libopenbios/bindings.h"
 #include "libopenbios/initprogram.h"
@@ -87,6 +89,69 @@
 	(void)feval("openbios-feval-selftest-no-such-word");
 }
 
+/* THE PRECISION FIX'S OWN FIXTURE SET, run inside the shipped firmware.
+ *
+ * libc/vsprintf.c's %s case treated `precision` as a MINIMUM. Six of the seven
+ * cases below were wrong before that was corrected, and the seventh -- plain
+ * %s, no precision -- is the must-NOT-break control: a "fix" that routed the
+ * no-precision path through strnlen(s, -1) would still pass the other six.
+ *
+ * Run in the FIRMWARE and not on the host on purpose. libc/vsprintf.c is the
+ * target's printf; kernel/bootstrap.c #defines printk to the HOST printf under
+ * BOOTSTRAP, so a host harness would test glibc and report on OpenBIOS. Case 3
+ * is the one the tree actually contains: fs/hfsplus/hfsp_record.c prints a
+ * FourCC that is NOT NUL-terminated with `%4.4s`.
+ *
+ * ( -- )  prints one line per case and a ratio; leaves the stack alone.
+ */
+static void printf_precision_selftest( void )
+{
+	/* Deliberately NOT NUL-terminated within its first four bytes: this is
+	 * the hfsplus shape, and it is what makes a strlen-based clip unsafe. */
+	static const char fourcc[] = { 'A','B','C','D','X','Y','Z','!','\0' };
+	static const char *abcdef = "abcdef";
+	static const char *abc = "abc";
+	char got[32];
+	int ok = 0, n = 0;
+
+	/* THE RETURNED LENGTH IS CHECKED TOO, and the control is why.
+	 *
+	 * strcmp() stops at the first NUL, so it cannot see an over-read that
+	 * copies PAST one. Measured with the defect re-injected: `%.10s` on
+	 * "abc" wrote ten bytes -- "abc", the terminator, and six bytes of
+	 * whatever followed -- and strcmp(got, "abc") said EQUAL. The case that
+	 * most directly demonstrates the bug was the one case the fixture could
+	 * not see. vsnprintf() returns str-buf, i.e. what it actually produced,
+	 * so the length catches it: 10 against an expected 3. */
+#define PREC_CASE(label, want, ...) do {				\
+		int rc_ = snprintf(got, sizeof(got), __VA_ARGS__);	\
+		int good_ = !strcmp(got, (want))			\
+			    && rc_ == (int)strlen(want);		\
+		n++;							\
+		if( good_ )						\
+			ok++;						\
+		printk("printf-precision: %s want[%s](%d) got[%s](%d)"	\
+		       " %s\n", (label), (want), (int)strlen(want),	\
+		       got, rc_, good_ ? "OK" : "BAD");			\
+	} while(0)
+
+	/* 1-2: precision clips, and does not extend. */
+	PREC_CASE("clip",      "abc",       "%.3s",   abcdef);
+	PREC_CASE("no-extend", "abc",       "%.10s",  abc);
+	/* 3: the in-tree case -- four bytes of a buffer with no NUL in them. */
+	PREC_CASE("fourcc",    "ABCD",      "%4.4s",  fourcc);
+	/* 4: MUST NOT BREAK -- no precision at all. */
+	PREC_CASE("bare",      "abc",       "%s",     abc);
+	/* 5: zero precision prints nothing. */
+	PREC_CASE("zero",      "",          "%.0s",   abc);
+	/* 6-7: precision and field width are different things. */
+	PREC_CASE("left",      "abc     |", "%-8.3s|", abcdef);
+	PREC_CASE("right",     "     abc|", "%8.3s|",  abcdef);
+#undef PREC_CASE
+
+	printk("printf-precision: %d/%d ok\n", ok, n);
+}
+
 void
 openbios_init( void )
 {
@@ -108,6 +173,9 @@
 	// The reporter's must-catch fixture -- see feval_report_selftest above
 	bind_func("test-feval-report", feval_report_selftest);
 
+	// libc/vsprintf.c's %s precision fixtures -- see above
+	bind_func("test-printf-precision", printf_precision_selftest);
+
 	// Bind the LE access words
 	bind_func("le-w!", lewstore);
 	bind_func("le-l!", lelstore);
